  "diagEnableBlockingBody": "When Freedom Control is on and Freedom is not earned, Daylock uses Accessibility to leave a selected app and show its own lock screen. Installed-app lists and selections stay on this device. It does not read screen content or collect browsing history. Only the latest selected-app detection and restriction result stay on this device. Other window package names are compared only to cancel a pending action if you switch apps; they are not retained. You can turn blocking off here or revoke Accessibility access at any time.\n\nFreedom inputs stay valid for their calendar date without reopening Daylock. If current state is missing or invalid, Daylock offers a recovery screen with Open Daylock and Disable Blocking; subsequent openings are allowed while recovery is needed. This is a voluntary restriction, not a tamper-proof or parental-control system.",
  "diagPermissionBody": "Daylock uses Android Accessibility to detect when a selected app opens, including while Daylock is in the background. It saves only the latest selected-app detection, Freedom status and restriction result on this device; nothing is sent to a server. Installed-app lists and selections stay on this device. It does not read screen content or collect browsing history. With the separate app restrictions switch enabled, it can return Home and show a branded Daylock lock screen when Freedom is not earned. Window package changes are compared only to cancel actions if you switch apps; other app names are not retained. It does not draw overlays or restrict unselected apps.\n\nYou choose whether to enable “Daylock app restrictions” in Android Accessibility Settings. You can disable it there at any time.",
